@display("Two layers of highlighting")
highlighting: section {
  | Highlighting comes in two layers. The first needs only the grammar, so every file is
  | coloured before any package loads; the second comes from the [schemas](#language.schemas.kinds.schema) in `import` and
  | says what each name is.

  static_highlighting: term {{
    Highlighting from the core grammar alone: names, `<flags>`, `@annotations`, strings
    and raw strings, comments, brace counts, `$` invocations and `#` links. One TextMate
    or tree-sitter grammar covers every file without loading a package.
  }}

  semantic_highlighting: term {{
    Highlighting from the [schemas](#language.schemas.kinds.schema) in `import`: whether
    a name is a known kind, flag or annotation and valid where it stands, whether
    `name: X` names a kind or a type, whether a tag names a kind in scope, and which
    grammar to inject into an embed. It maps onto LSP semantic tokens and language
    injection.
  }}

  static_layer: rule(static_highlighting, by = product.system.language_server) {
    must {{ colour every file by one grammar of the language that reads no package, [the editor grammar](#product.system.editor_grammar) }}
  }

  | | A name that resolves to | Token class   | Modifier                                |
  | | ----------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------- |
  | | a kind                  | `class`       | `defaultLibrary` when `std/` declares it |
  | | an enum                 | `enum`        | as above                                |
  | | an interface            | `interface`   | as above                                |
  | | a type or a primitive   | `type`        | as above                                |
  | | an annotation           | `decorator`   | as above                                |
  | | a flag                  | `modifier`    | as above                                |
  | | a func, task, op, macro or [hook](#language.scripting.code.hook) | `function` | as above                         |
  | | a section, package or api | `namespace` | as above                                |
  | | a variant of an enum    | `enumMember`  | as above                                |
  | | a name being declared   | its class     | `declaration`                           |
  | | words in `///` lines    | `comment`     | `documentation`                         |

  semantic_layer: rule(semantic_highlighting, by = product.system.language_server) {
    must {{ classify each name of a file by what it resolves to in the checked tree, as the table gives, and leave a name that resolves to nothing to the static layer }}
  }

  token_override: rule(semantic_highlighting, by = product.system.language_server) {
    where {{ a kind, flag, annotation or language is declared with `@highlight(token = t)` }}
    should {{ give the names that resolve to it the token class `t` instead of the table's }}
  }

  injection: rule(semantic_highlighting, by = product.system.language_server) {
    where {{ an [embedded string](#language.grammar.strings.embedded.embedded_string) or a raw body is [cast](#language.grammar.code.expressions.cast) to a `language` declared with `grammar = g` }}
    should {{ inject the grammar `g` into its text, as `sh: language(grammar = "source.shell")` colours `(sh)"ls -la"` as shell }}
  }

  insert_safety: rule(semantic_highlighting, by = product.system.interpreter) {
    where {{ `@insert(mode)` stands above a `language` }}
    must {{ make values inserted into its embeds safe the way the mode says: `QUOTE` quotes each value for the language, `LITERAL` inserts it as written }}
    unless {{ `@insert<raw>` stands above the node that inserts }}
    example {{ `@insert(QUOTE) sh: language(grammar = "source.shell")` makes `(sh)"rm ${path}"` safe for a path with spaces }}
  }
}
